ow aligned with his ruling (2026-11-08)\n1029:## 29. Test set, once for c4 (`test-c4-flash/`): the line is met (one draw)\n1060:## 30. The c4 dev run stopped on a cut answer; the runner misread Alex's retry rule; fixed and continued (2026-11-08)\n1087:## 31. Whole bench for c4 (dev, one draw) and later linking checked (2026-11-08)\n1131:## 32. Step 2 re-measured on the c4 records; Jev's criteria aligned with the merge line (2026-11-08)\n1174:## 33. Arm c5: one party's statement per item (2026-11-08)\n1200:## 34. c5 measured; Step 2 lines 1–2 met on its records; c6 narrows the rule and raises the output cap (2026-11-08)\n1250:## 35. c6 measured: Step 2 lines 1–2 met; Step 1 misses only \"no fragments\" by one per set; the question gold is far stricter than the readers (2026-11-08)\n1311:## 36. Alex decides: open only big matters, by the frozen gold's standard (2026-11-08)\n1328:## 37. Arm c7: the opening rule from the standard's closed set (2026-11-08)\n1369:## 38. c7 test, once; question measure baselines (2026-11-08)\n1398:## 39. Alex: \"你这些改动有我你有好好的跑council 再下手\"; retro council on the unreviewed changes (2026-11-08)\n1441:## 40. Council: complete review of the proposed final configuration (2026-11-08; Alex: \"你的goal 需要严格的跑council 来review 和思考再决策\")\n1494:## 41. c8: whole-request audit, check sends, the checks before freezing, and the acceptance set (2026-11-08/09)\n1564:## 42. Acceptance set chosen and readers' lists frozen; runs launched (2026-11-09)\n1603:## 43. Acceptance on the fresh set: every line met; links are the weak point (2026-11-09)\n1645:## 44. Dev ×3, the reflection council, and the link recheck (2026-11-09)\n1696:## 45. Alex adopts c8 (D387); the PRD carries it; next, the account of weak links (2026-11-09)\n1721:## 46. Cause of link clutter: the model's own account; council stops a send (2026-11-09)\n1750:## 47. Alex approves the link-check design; reflection council on the day; code review and cleanup for check-in (2026-11-09)\n1822:## 48. Commit; the Ohio-guarantee miss diagnosed; one mechanism under two classes (2026-11-09)\n1863:## 49. Arm c9 (the \"covers\" split) probed and stopped (2026-11-09)\n1901:## 50. The failed c9 answers read against the source ($0; Alex: \"先把失败的回答对照原文看清\")\n1948:## 51. Arms c10a and c10b (the D388 read-back) probed and stopped (2026-11-09)\n2020:## 52. Arm c11 (a required company-impact field) probed and stopped (2026-11-09)\n2065:## 53. Deep council on the D388 line (c9–c11), on Alex's word \"run council to review deeply\" (2026-11-09)\n2132:## 54. The focused call for D388 probed and stopped (2026-11-09)\n2194:## 55. Focused call, round 2 (two named causes fixed) probed and stopped (2026-11-09)\n2229:## 56. Focused call, round 3 (D389: 5% of annual profit) probed and stopped on one case (2026-11-09)\n2268:## 57. The programme-versus-deal fix: three drafts blocked; the boundary goes to Alex (2026-11-09)","numLines":58,"totalLines":58}}
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/round-1.jsonl:39:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgrSeKcR3dthPoQHNAwFD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Tn1pyarmYA5KWGY1Q6pQjq","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md","offset":1603,"limit":95},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2554,"cache_read_input_tokens":90787,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2554},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c06c24f-8f77-4add-9443-7081a27b7903","uuid":"63e659a9-e871-4152-82a6-df5db0cd8c5a","timestamp":"2026-11-09T00:55:21.627Z","request_id":"req_011CgrSeKQFnimcQ6DCyoEnW","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Tn1pyarmYA5KWGY1Q6pQjq":{"file_path":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md","offset":1603,"limit":95}}}
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/round-1.jsonl:40:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tn1pyarmYA5KWGY1Q6pQjq","type":"tool_result","content":"1603\t## 43. Acceptance on the fresh set: every line met; links are the weak point (2026-11-09)\n1604\t\n1605\tThe fresh Step 1 run finished: 50 of 50 articles complete in 52 calls. Two format failures were resent once and passed; in one of\n1606\tthem the model had added a field the schema does not have.\n1607\t- Cost: $0.66 at published rates (input $0.3/M, output $1.2/M, no cache share applied).\n1608\t- 188 kept items and 10 opened theses.\n1609\t- Step 2 placed the 188 records in 131 groups (57 repeats, 0 refused). Later linking judged 182 pairs and linked 1.\n1610\t\n1611\t**Readers.** Two readers per batch of ten articles, and a third reader on the 23 disputes (8 matters, 15 links). On the two articles\n1612\tI had traced myself (9 and 42) the readers agree with me.\n1613\t\n1614\t| Line | Result | Pass |\n1615\t|---|---|---|\n1616\t| Helpful matters kept | 132 / 139 = **95.0%** (core 83 / 83). All 7 misses are supporting: two author estimates, Huang's agent-compute claim (kept in another article), the capital-return policy, a driver exploit report, handheld openness, a Kioxia SSD | ≥ 81.2%: **met** |\n1617\t| Wrongly opened theses | **1** of 10, by both readers: clean-5, \"will Vera Rubin account for about 20% of data-center revenue in fiscal Q3\", a revenue share and so a financial measure (D382) | ≤ 2: **met** |\n1618\t| Search | **141 / 143** settled repeat pairs = 98.6%; 2 pairs not exercised because a member was not kept | ≥ 90%: **met** |\n1619\t| Wrong merges | **1 / 57** = 1.8%, none unjudged. 52 were accepted by both readers; of the 5 disputed, the third reader found 2 acceptable, 2 overlap and 1 wrong (Vera Rubin deliveries merged into production status and revenue share) | ≤ 2%: **met** |\n1620\t| Detail retention | grouped **22 / 26** = ungrouped 22 / 26. This is structural: all 188 records are stored with their own text. The 4 details missing are Step 1 omissions | no fewer: **met** |\n1621\t\n1622\t**My disagreement with the readers on theses.** I would also call two theses wrong:\n1623\t- clean-9, global semiconductor revenue in 2026 (Deloitte's estimate): an industry aggregate that adds little beyond NVIDIA's own\n1624\t  guidance;\n1625\t- clean-10, NAND demand created by NVIDIA's CMX: a memory supplier's quantity.\n1626\t\n1627\tBoth readers judged them shared quantities that the standard allows. By my reading the count is 3, which misses the line. This is\n1628\treported both ways.\n1629\t\n1630\t**Reported without a line.**\n1631\t- **Kept items.** Both readers called 138 helpful, 20 noise and 13 optional; the rest were split.\n1632\t- **Links: 59 / 93 judged right (63%).** This is the weak point. I read all 34 links that were not judged right:\n1633\t  - about 25 are real errors, where an item is linked to the topically nearest open thesis without bearing on its question. Examples:\n1634\t    SK Hynix's Indiana plant linked to NVIDIA's supplier commitments; a Fed-cut probability linked to hyperscaler capex; Wistron's\n1635\t    share sale linked to Vera Rubin's revenue share; Micron's revenue linked to NVIDIA's commitments;\n1636\t  - about 9 look like reader strictness. Examples: the acquisition announcement that founded the Hugging Face thesis; memory-cost\n1637\t    margin pressure linked to the server DRAM price thesis.\n1638\t- **The class in production.** It is production's open defect 1, where news is filed under the nearest revenue question (D355),\n1639\t  and the later-link disagreement of §41.\n1640\t\n1641\t**Next class (for the council):** link precision. First, a $0 check on the dev draws:\n1642\t- links per item and the share of items linked to two or more theses, c8 against c7;\n1643\t- whether c8's \"link one statement to every thesis it informs\" (an audit amendment) raised over-linking.\n1644\t\n1645\t## 44. Dev ×3, the reflection council, and the link recheck (2026-11-09)\n1646\t\n1647\t**Dev ×3 of the frozen c8** (115 dev articles, an empty start, each draw complete: 116, 115 and 117 calls; frozen golden-v5 dev,\n1648\tsha `6f9476bb…`, scored as a lead):\n1649\t\n1650\t| Measure | Draw 1 | Draw 2 | Draw 3 | c7 (earlier) |\n1651\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1652\t| Helpful, of 234 (line 190) | 214 | 211 | 212 | 212 |\n1653\t| Core, of 86 | 85 | 86 | 86 | 86 |\n1654\t| Not-kept spans kept | 27 | 27 | 30 | 30 |\n1655\t| Theses opened | 8 | 10 | 10 | 10 |\n1656\t| must_open / may_open / none / must_not (one reader) | 4/3/1/0 | 3/3/3/1 (lidar) | 3/2/5/0 | 4/2/3/1 |\n1657\t| must_open covered, of 28 | 25 | 24 | 27 | 27 |\n1658\t| Search (reference mapped onto the draw) | 98/99 = 99.0% | 91/92 = 98.9% | 97/97 = 100% | |\n1659\t| Clear wrong merges (third reader) | 1/100 | 1/100 | 1/106 | |\n1660\t| Overlaps | 14 | 12 | 15 | |\n1661\t\n1662\t- **Stable across the three draws:** the six core theses (credit support, Vera Rubin, China H200, hyperscaler capex, Kyber, supply\n1663\t  commitments).\n1664\t- **Varies:** the edges (memory prices, Rubin Ultra, China budget shift, lidar, DeepSeek inquiry, custom ASIC pool, inference-chip\n1665\t  startups).\n1666\t- **Not covered in any draw:** the Ohio-guarantee yes/no question. The broader credit-support amount thesis is opened instead.\n1667\t\n1668\t**Reflection council** (a fresh thread, `council/ap-codex.md`, then `aq-codex.md`). It confirmed helpful, search and theses. It\n1669\tretracted its first claim of 3/57 wrong merges after reading the third reader's prompt: `merge_acceptable: false` is forced on\n1670\toverlaps, which the prompt defines as \"not misleading\", and the agreed line is clear wrong merges (D381). Agreed caveats:\n1671\t- report search both ways: 141/143 for search alone, 141/145 (97.2%) for the whole system. One 11-member group supplies 55 pairs, and\n1672\t  revisions are barely exercised;\n1673\t- line 3 is a storage comparison, not a usability test;\n1674\t- my thesis dissent is recorded as a breadth concern, not a count: the council reads clean-9 and clean-10 as supported shared\n1675\t  quantities;\n1676\t- c8 did not raise links per item, but that says nothing about whether the links are right.\n1677\t\n1678\tVerdict: REPORT AS STATED.\n1679\t\n1680\t**Link recheck (the council's cheapest next step).** A fresh senior-analyst reader saw the 34 links not judged right, mixed blind\n1681\twith 10 links judged right, and asked whether the item helps assess the question:\n1682\t\n1683\t| Original verdict | Useful | Weak | Wrong |\n1684\t|---|---|---|---|\n1685\t| Not right (34) | 13 | 19 | 2 |\n1686\t| Right, as controls (10) | 10 | 0 | 0 |\n1687\t\n1688\tOver all 93 links, about 72 are useful (77%), 19 are weak (20%: related, but they would not change the view) and 2 are wrong (2%).\n1689\tThe link problem is mainly clutter, not misleading filing. My earlier estimate of 25 real errors is withdrawn.\n1690\t\n1691\t**Next, for Alex:**\n1692\t- whether to adopt c8 as the clean Step 1 configuration (PRD §12);\n1693\t- whether link clutter, about one link in five, is worth a round. If it is, the round starts with the model's own account on weak\n1694\t  links, a few cents, before any design.\n1695\t\n1696\t## 45. Alex adopts c8 (D387); the PRD carries it; next, the account of weak links (2026-11-09)\n1697\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c06c24f-8f77-4add-9443-7081a27b7903","uuid":"2c2ad6f9-4b05-4544-84f8-5283f6e5411e","timestamp":"2026-11-09T00:55:21.631Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md","content":"## 43. Acceptance on the fresh set: every line met; links are the weak point (2026-11-09)\n\nThe fresh Step 1 run finished: 50 of 50 articles complete in 52 calls. Two format failures were resent once and passed; in one of\nthem the model had added a field the schema does not have.\n- Cost: $0.66 at published rates (input $0.3/M, output $1.2/M, no cache share applied).\n- 188 kept items and 10 opened theses.\n- Step 2 placed the 188 records in 131 groups (57 repeats, 0 refused). Later linking judged 182 pairs and linked 1.\n\n**Readers.** Two readers per batch of ten articles, and a third reader on the 23 disputes (8 matters, 15 links). On the two articles\nI had traced myself (9 and 42) the readers agree with me.\n\n| Line | Result | Pass |\n|---|---|---|\n| Helpful matters kept | 132 / 139 = **95.0%** (core 83 / 83). All 7 misses are supporting: two author estimates, Huang's agent-compute claim (kept in another article), the capital-return policy, a driver exploit report, handheld openness, a Kioxia SSD | ≥ 81.2%: **met** |\n| Wrongly opened theses | **1** of 10, by both readers: clean-5, \"will Vera Rubin account for about 20% of data-center revenue in fiscal Q3\", a revenue share and so a financial measure (D382) | ≤ 2: **met** |\n| Search | **141 / 143** settled repeat pairs = 98.6%; 2 pairs not exercised because a member was not kept | ≥ 90%: **met** |\n| Wrong merges | **1 / 57** = 1.8%, none unjudged. 52 were accepted by both readers; of the 5 disputed, the third reader found 2 acceptable, 2 overlap and 1 wrong (Vera Rubin deliveries merged into production status and revenue share) | ≤ 2%: **met** |\n| Detail retention | grouped **22 / 26** = ungrouped 22 / 26. This is structural: all 188 records are stored with their own text. The 4 details missing are Step 1 omissions | no fewer: **met** |\n\n**My disagreement with the readers on theses.** I would also call two theses wrong:\n- clean-9, global semiconductor revenue in 2026 (Deloitte's estimate): an industry aggregate that adds little beyond NVIDIA's own\n  guidance;\n- clean-10, NAND demand created by NVIDIA's CMX: a memory supplier's quantity.\n\nBoth readers judged them shared quantities that the standard allows. By my reading the count is 3, which misses the line. This is\nreported both ways.\n\n**Reported without a line.**\n- **Kept items.** Both readers called 138 helpful, 20 noise and 13 optional; the rest were split.\n- **Links: 59 / 93 judged right (63%).** This is the weak point. I read all 34 links that were not judged right:\n  - about 25 are real errors, where an item is linked to the topically nearest open thesis without bearing on its question. Examples:\n    SK Hynix's Indiana plant linked to NVIDIA's supplier commitments; a Fed-cut probability linked to hyperscaler capex; Wistron's\n    share sale linked to Vera Rubin's revenue share; Micron's revenue linked to NVIDIA's commitments;\n  - about 9 look like reader strictness. Examples: the acquisition announcement that founded the Hugging Face thesis; memory-cost\n    margin pressure linked to the server DRAM price thesis.\n- **The class in production.** It is production's open defect 1, where news is filed under the nearest revenue question (D355),\n  and the later-link disagreement of §41.\n\n**Next class (for the council):** link precision. First, a $0 check on the dev draws:\n- links per item and the share of items linked to two or more theses, c8 against c7;\n- whether c8's \"link one statement to every thesis it informs\" (an audit amendment) raised over-linking.\n\n## 44. Dev ×3, the reflection council, and the link recheck (2026-11-09)\n\n**Dev ×3 of the frozen c8** (115 dev articles, an empty start, each draw complete: 116, 115 and 117 calls; frozen golden-v5 dev,\nsha `6f9476bb…`, scored as a lead):\n\n| Measure | Draw 1 | Draw 2 | Draw 3 | c7 (earlier) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Helpful, of 234 (line 190) | 214 | 211 | 212 | 212 |\n| Core, of 86 | 85 | 86 | 86 | 86 |\n| Not-kept spans kept | 27 | 27 | 30 | 30 |\n| Theses opened | 8 | 10 | 10 | 10 |\n| must_open / may_open / none / must_not (one reader) | 4/3/1/0 | 3/3/3/1 (lidar) | 3/2/5/0 | 4/2/3/1 |\n| must_open covered, of 28 | 25 | 24 | 27 | 27 |\n| Search (reference mapped onto the draw) | 98/99 = 99.0% | 91/92 = 98.9% | 97/97 = 100% | |\n| Clear wrong merges (third reader) | 1/100 | 1/100 | 1/106 | |\n| Overlaps | 14 | 12 | 15 | |\n\n- **Stable across the three draws:** the six core theses (credit support, Vera Rubin, China H200, hyperscaler capex, Kyber, supply\n  commitments).\n- **Varies:** the edges (memory prices, Rubin Ultra, China budget shift, lidar, DeepSeek inquiry, custom ASIC pool, inference-chip\n  startups).\n- **Not covered in any draw:** the Ohio-guarantee yes/no question. The broader credit-support amount thesis is opened instead.\n\n**Reflection council** (a fresh thread, `council/ap-codex.md`, then `aq-codex.md`). It confirmed helpful, search and theses. It\nretracted its first claim of 3/57 wrong merges after reading the third reader's prompt: `merge_acceptable: false` is forced on\noverlaps, which the prompt defines as \"not misleading\", and the agreed line is clear wrong merges (D381). Agreed caveats:\n- report search both ways: 141/143 for search alone, 141/145 (97.2%) for the whole system. One 11-member group supplies 55 pairs, and\n  revisions are barely exercised;\n- line 3 is a storage comparison, not a usability test;\n- my thesis dissent is recorded as a breadth concern, not a count: the council reads clean-9 and clean-10 as supported shared\n  quantities;\n- c8 did not raise links per item, but that says nothing about whether the links are right.\n\nVerdict: REPORT AS STATED.\n\n**Link recheck (the council's cheapest next step).** A fresh senior-analyst reader saw the 34 links not judged right, mixed blind\nwith 10 links judged right, and asked whether the item helps assess the question:\n\n| Original verdict | Useful | Weak | Wrong |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Not right (34) | 13 | 19 | 2 |\n| Right, as controls (10) | 10 | 0 | 0 |\n\nOver all 93 links, about 72 are useful (77%), 19 are weak (20%: related, but they would not change the view) and 2 are wrong (2%).\nThe link problem is mainly clutter, not misleading filing. My earlier estimate of 25 real errors is withdrawn.\n\n**Next, for Alex:**\n- whether to adopt c8 as the clean Step 1 configuration (PRD §12);\n- whether link clutter, about one link in five, is worth a round. If it is, the round starts with the model's own account on weak\n  links, a few cents, before any design.\n\n## 45. Alex adopts c8 (D387); the PRD carries it; next, the account of weak links (2026-11-09)\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1603,"totalLines":2286}}}
