744:Before you return the answer, check it once. Read each evidence item back as the analyst following the company and keep it when you would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely, about the company or its customers, suppliers, competitors, regulators or the economy. Ask first whether it is new: repeated common knowledge, a long-standing situation retold and an unargued view are noise. Do not drop a reported step, figure, guidance, forecast or decision because the item retells it or another outlet reported it before: a later step compares each statement with the evidence already stored and records a repeat there. Guidance, a forecast, a plan or a decision still in force keeps its place even when retold, including a change to a plan, design or schedule that another outlet reported days or weeks before. Judge the action or information itself, not its verb tense, and do not take a statement as old because its date is missing. Fitting one of the four kinds does not keep an item. A missing amount, date or scale never drops an item on its own: a forecast, a rumour, a counter-example or a distant or conditional statement is judged like any other.
748:- a product, game, driver or benchmark specification with no business scale ("the vacuum runs 60 minutes on a charge"; "a benchmark score"), a retail price detail ("a retailer marks one model down by $50"), and a comparison the company, a partner or a third party publishes ("the maker says its drug works twice as fast as older ones"; "a reviewer's test"); it does not cover a competitor's comparison against the company's product, a partner's or customer's adoption or integration (also when a comparison shares its sentence), a reported launch, shipment, delay or cancellation of any of the company's products or a change to a product plan or configuration, including a reported sign that a planned product is going ahead ("the long-range version of the jet is likely delayed or limited to a few deliveries"; "a parts supplier's catalogue lists the unannounced model"), nor the company's decision to change the price it charges; uncertainty or a smaller product line does not make it a specification;
753:- the same fact said again in other words within this item: one deal, hire, programme or reported measure told in several sentences, with its terms, figures and growth rates, is one evidence item; keep the fuller wording ("a $2 billion order for 120 stores in 15 countries over three years"; "cloud revenue of $8 billion, up 30% on the year and 6% on the quarter, 40% of the total"); a deal one party signs under a wider programme, with its own amount or capacity, is its own evidence item, and a fact another outlet reported before is not a repeat within this item;
1078:Alex approved returning the evidence tier, original statement date and described period in Step 1. The draft also identifies the original party and reporting speaker separately. Mapping these fields to the existing writer and readers remains to be verified, including how source-specific attribution and tier are retained when a repeat adds another source. The signed historical standard uses more specific opening categories and check-date rules; Alex has clarified that the clean design may open a meaningful topic without a known decision date when later observable developments can help assess it. Other differences remain subject to audit. This draft must not be described as fully compatible with that standard or the production schema without a separate review.
1163:The review covered the visible system prompt, sample input, proposed schema and sample response together, with an independent reader. It found three places needing more than formatting: the thesis bar did not explain which business outcome the question could change; the within-item repeat instruction could hide distinct useful facts; and the output did not distinguish the original party from the reporting speaker. The draft above addresses those points. Its expanded examples teach these distinctions across industries; their added length is intentional for that purpose, not a measured quality improvement.
1165:As historical context, two saved responses and their input articles were read in the local [older-run archive](../poc/data/e2e-v83-seed-d2/archive.sqlite): call 4 records two descriptions of an investment in the same article, while call 7 reports a company's plan alongside a separate party's capacity figures. These are examples from an older request, not outputs of this draft. They illustrate why the new request must distinguish repeated descriptions from distinct facts and the original party from the reporter; they do not establish that the revised prompt fixes either case.
